{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788304951,"nonce":"vuY+XLRBToX4MYENFd1EB5BXECE=","message":"ONE OBSERVATION CARRIED OVER from `8799218`, filed independently by the `1113019` worker and closed as a duplicate of this issue.\n\nIt reached the same finding from the opposite end — implementing the day-long default bound and asking what makes a day safe to sleep through — and it contributes something this issue's body does not state:\n\n**`CheckUntilKeys` refuses a SECOND matcher.** So the opt-in halts are not merely optional, they are **mutually exclusive**: an agent already waiting on `until:{condition}` cannot also ask to be woken by a raid. It must choose one question.\n\nThat strengthens the case in this issue's Scope from \"make the halts unconditional so a sleeping agent is woken\" to \"make them unconditional **because there is no way for a caller to ask for more than one**\". A workaround does not exist even for an agent that knows the hazard and wants to guard against it.\n\nWorth an acceptance bullet, and adding one: **an unconditional letter/alert halt fires while an `until:{condition}` advance is armed** — i.e. the two do not compete, and arming a condition does not cost the agent its wake-ups. That is the case `CheckUntilKeys` makes unreachable today.","files":null}]}